at last on a sunny day in london  a breakthrough after years of  negotiation a deal to prevent the  world's tech giants from avoiding  billions of dollars of taxes  a deal many people thought would simply  never happen  it's a very proud moment whether it's  people operating in tax havens or as  digital companies we're going to level  the playing field and inject that  principle of  fairness into our global tax system the  details are complex but the deal g7  finance ministers have agreed  comes down to two key reforms a global  minimum and corporate tax rate of 15  percent to prevent companies like google  facebook and apple  shifting profits to low tax  jurisdictions and new rules to calculate  what they owe  and where while it could involve  billions more pounds of revenues  it's still unclear whether those rules  for instance would apply to amazon  now this might have happened in the uk  but the main reason it's happened at all  is because of pressure from the white  house and u.s treasury secretary janet  yellen  what you're seeing is a revival  of multilateralism a willingness  of leading nations in the g7  and the g20 uh to cooperate  to address the most critical challenges  facing the global economy and this  international  tax race to the bottom has been  a problem for many many countries for  decades  it represents a real watershed the last  time there was a global deal  on business taxation was all the way  back a century ago by the league of  nations so while there's still some  question marks about the ambitiousness  of this  there's no doubting the momentousness of  it but still there  are a couple of questions first of all  how will this actually work in practice  secondly beyond the g7 will other  countries  actually sign up to it ireland has drawn  in billions of euros of investment  thanks in part to its famously low  corporation tax of 12 and a half percent  but that rate is below the floor the g7  agreed meaning says its finance minister  a significant impact on his country well  from the modeling that we have been  doing  uh we have indicated that it could  affect up to one-fifth of our corporate  tax revenue  but what we've also been doing is we've  been planning for this  so those kind of revenue losses are  already  included in our budgetary calculations  of course  that depends on this deal getting  approval from the rest of the developed  world  that's not a given but the oecd the  institution which effectively wrote this  tax template  is confident today there was a very  broad consensus and there's more work to  be done there will be more conversations  and  in the end it's important to have a  global taxation system that is that is  overall fair  in theory this deal could bring in  billions for the uk in theory  it could stem the tide of tax avoidance  in theory it could change the  relationship between big business  and the state forever in theory  but here in london what once looked only  theoretical  has become that bit more real  ed conway sky news
 

